<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Tokushima Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1585 CE – 1871 CE · capital Tokushima</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The domain of the Hachisuka family in Awa on Shikoku, which grew rich on indigo and gave Japan the Awa Odori dance festival.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a> in 1585, he granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awa-province-tokushima">Awa</a> province to his retainer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hachisuka-iemasa">Hachisuka Iemasa</a>, who built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokushima">Tokushima</a> castle. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hachisuka-yoshishige">Yoshishige</a> fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> in 1600 and in 1615 the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awaji-island">Awaji</a> was added, bringing the domain to about 257,000 koku.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hachisuka-clan">Hachisuka</a> lords ruled for the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. The domain promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> cultivation on the Yoshino river plain and controlled its sale, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awa-province-tokushima">Awa</a> indigo famous across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, while salt pans also brought revenue. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awa-odori">Awa Odori</a> summer dance, traditionally linked to the castle's completion, became the domain's signature festival, and puppet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> flourished.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The domain supported the new government in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>. A violent dispute with the Inada family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awaji-island">Awaji</a> in 1870 led to Awaji's transfer, and after abolition in 1871 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awa-province-tokushima">Awa</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokushima-prefecture">Tokushima Prefecture</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tokushima-domain.jpg" alt="Tokushima Domain" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: by Reggaeman · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tokushima_castle_03.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1585 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tokushima</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hachisuka Iemasa, Hachisuka Yoshishige, Hachisuka Mochiaki</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Japan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Awa Domain, Hachisuka Domain</dd></dl></aside></div>
